# Tracer: verifier-produced proof-rule IR preserves equality transport.
#
# Before (did not work):
# abstract_prop transported(a)
# forall a, b set:
#     $transported(a)
#     a = b
#     =>:
#         $transported(b)
#
# Former behavior: Litex verified the forall, but flattened the conclusion to
# a citation of `$transported(a)`. To-Lean then failed with "has no stored
# FactId" and had no record that `a = b` transported the cited fact to the
# target `$transported(b)`.
#
# Now (verified):
abstract_prop transported(a)

forall a, b set:
    $transported(a)
    a = b
    =>:
        $transported(b)

# Broader first-slice coverage:
abstract_prop marked(x)

prop is_one(x R):
    x = 1

trust forall x R:
    x != 0
    =>:
        $marked(x)

$is_one(1)

forall x R:
    x != 0
    x < 10
    =>:
        $marked(x)

forall x R:
    x != 0
    =>:
        x != 0

forall a, b, x R:
    x != 0
    =>:
        (a + b) / x = a / x + b / x
# Current behavior: the verifier retains the ordered equality path and local
# premise FactIds. To-Lean represents the conclusion as a `RuleApplication`
# containing `EqualityRewrite`, a cited source-fact premise, and equality-proof
# premises. The same extensible rule node also represents definition reduction,
# known-forall application, and normalization. Only explicit `trust` becomes
# `axiom`.
#
# Boundary: transports without structured verifier evidence (currently, for
# example, order-predicate transposition) become `OtherUnsupported` and stop
# compilation. Directly compiling `$marked(1)` also still requires a nested
# numeric proof backend. Neither boundary becomes `sorry` or an implicit axiom.
#
# Evidence: target/release/litex -compact -isolated -runner -f examples/01_proof_patterns/to_lean_ir_mvp.lit
# Compiler evidence: cargo test --release to_lean:: -- --nocapture
# Implementation: src/environment/known_equality.rs,
# src/verify/verify_known_atomic_facts.rs, src/result/runtime_success.rs,
# src/to_lean_ir, src/runtime/runtime_to_lean_ir.rs, src/to_lean
